The Key Divisions Between Realism and Liberalism
Human Nature and Power
One of the central divisions between realism and liberalism lies in their views on human nature and
its implications for power and international relations.
Realism: A Pessimistic View of Human Nature
Realists, drawing heavily on the ideas of thinkers like Thomas Hobbes and Hans Morgenthau, view
human nature as inherently selfish, competitive, and power-hungry. According to realism, humans
are primarily driven by fear, self-interest, and the desire for power, which translates directly into the
behaviour of states. Because states are composed of individuals, their actions reflect the same
basic instincts—self-preservation, mistrust of others, and competition for dominance.
Realists argue that this pessimistic view of human nature makes conflict and competition
inevitable. States, operating in an anarchic international system with no overarching authority to
enforce rules, must prioritise their survival above all else. This requires constant vigilance, a focus
on accumulating power, and a readiness to use force when necessary. For realists, power—often
in the form of military strength—is the only reliable currency in international relations. It ensures a
state’s survival and allows it to influence others.
Liberalism: An Optimistic View of Human Nature
Liberalism, in contrast, presents a more optimistic and cooperative view of human nature,
influenced by thinkers like John Locke. Liberals argue that humans are rational and capable of
